A||||13||Sootah||HELP!! How do you remove water deposits from the paint and glass?||||5||
Z||000000||Sootah||10-08-2001||10:28 PM||||As mentioned in my other rather excited thread, I just got me a sweet black '85 <B>GT</B>. The guy bought it as a restore project so it sat for a while, few months. Its parking spot was adjacent to the lawn and he has the mist type sprinklers so there is a layer of water deposits all over the car. Just exactly like what you would find on the inside of your shower.<P>I am picking the car up Tuesday or Wednesday and I am going to take it to a good car wash, so that may take it all off. I'm just asking now in case it doesnt. Any other tips on cleaning it up? I've got some polishing compound as well as Turtle Wax. Gonna clean it, polish, clean, wax. Tips?<P>I also need seat covers. Classic drivers seat rip. What ones fit best? Or how much would it cost to reupholster them?<P>The sail panels are gone. Dude said they were totally shattered, so he took them off. Was gonna replace but now its mine so I've gotta do it myself. Have a post in the mall bout it.<P>One more thing now: The jockey box cover on the center console is gone. I need the vinyl/cloth dealie that covers the front of it. If you don't know what I am talking about I will post a pic.<P><B><I>PEACE -Sootah</B></I><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">    <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B>WANTED</B>: Aero Fiero in good condition, Manual, V6 preferred.</FONT>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Sootah||10-08-2001||10:30 PM||||Guess I can remove the wanted thing out of my sig. Got exactly what I wanted (and more!!). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Damn I'm happy.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroMaster88||10-08-2001||11:20 PM||||Well..if it doesnt come off the paint after a good cleaning try some bug and tar remover.  That stuff works on ANYTHING!  For the windows try some windex or some CLR.  Should work good.  Have fun with your new Fiero.  Good luck!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">              <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pike||10-08-2001||11:38 PM||||I had the same problem with my black 87 GT. I tried everything but was unsuccessful until...<P>get yourself a cloth, about the size of a handtowel. wet it thoroughly. get some of the canned past mild rubbing compound. apply the compound the compound with the wet cloth. It will go on fast and with a little elbow grease all the water spots will be gone. After compound dries, remopve with a soft diaper or buffer. The bonus is that you also remove a tun of scratches and oxydation and the paint will look great. Make sure you select a mild or stage 1 compound. <BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pike||10-08-2001||11:40 PM||||OH...I forgot to mention, it works great on the glass too! Makes it look purdy!<BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||10-08-2001||11:56 PM||||When I had my car painted, I got water deposits on all my glass.  It had clearcoat and paint in the water, so it wouldn't come off.  I used 1500-2000 grit wet-sandpaper; worked great!  Didn't scratch too bad either.  I think 2000 should be fine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroSoul||10-09-2001||06:48 AM||||For the glass try RainX 2000 glass cleaner. Good stuff and no after film.  I would try a cleaner wax like Meguires on the finish.  Good Luck.||||reg||6
Z||000007||mrfixit58||10-09-2001||09:19 AM||||Like Pike, I used Turtle Wax Rubbing Compound. I used a power buffer on the sunroof but first removed it from the car and removed all the fastening hardware. It came out beautiful.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000008||Sootah||10-09-2001||10:20 AM||||I've got Turtle Wax Polishing Compound. Isn't polishing compound a mild rubbing compound?<P>Off to work I go, thanx 4 the tips. Bump<sup>1</sup>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||10-09-2001||11:18 AM||||Meguire's "Body Scrub" may work on the body. The Rain-X glass Polish does an excellent job of cleaning glass. It even removes some of the small scratches left by bad dew wipes.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Sootah||10-09-2001||10:16 PM||||Bump<sup>2</sup>||||reg||4
Z||000011||Pondski||10-10-2001||01:16 AM||||Have you tried Limeaway?  Do they still make that stuff? It's used for removing mineral deposits.  I have also used Dow Tub and Tile Cleaner and that gets the water deposits off of the glass everytime! ||||reg||6
Z||000012||Sootah||10-10-2001||04:28 AM||||My main concern is the paint. Glass should be a cinch as I can use pretty much whatever I damn well please on it. Don't wanna damage the paint though (It's just s' blasted purdy)..||||reg||4
Z||000013||RossT||10-10-2001||04:42 AM||||Soot.. I would use a claybar!!! not all claybars are created equal.  I use the one from <A HREF="http://www.zainobros.com" TARGET=_blank>www.zainobros.com</A>   They also make a glass product that removes just about anthing from glass, including very small scratches.   I have used claybar several times with great luck, just make sure you read the instructions first!!||||reg||1
